\subsection{$k$-means Clustering}
We have been somewhat lax in defining exactly \textit{what} a clustering algorithm should return as an answer. One possibility is to label each point with the name of the cluster that it is in. If there are $k$ clusters, these labels can be the integers 1 through $k$, where labeling point $p$ with $i$ means it is in the $i$th cluster. An equivalent output representation might be $k$ separate lists of points, where list $i$ represents all the points in the $i$th cluster.

But a more abstract notion reports the \textit{center point} of each cluster. Typically we think of natural clusters as compact, Gaussian-like regions, where there is an ideal center defining the location where the points ``should'' be. Given the set of these centers, clustering the points becomes easy: simply assign each point $p$ to the center point $C_{i}$ closest to it. The $i$th cluster consists of all points whose nearest center is $C_{i}$.

\textit{$k$-means clustering} is a fast, simple-to-understand, and generally effective approach to clustering. It starts by making a guess as to where the cluster centers might be, evaluates the quality of these centers, and then refines them to make better center estimates.

