\subsection{Generating Graphs}

\textbf{Input description}: Parameters describing the desired graph, including the number of vertices $n$, and the number of edges $m$ or edge probability $p$.

\textbf{Problem description}: Generate (1) all, or (2) a random, or (3) the next graph satisfying the parameters.

\textbf{Discussion}: Graph generation typically arises when constructing test data for programs. Perhaps you have two different programs that solve the same problem, and you want to see which is faster, or make sure that they both give the same (presumably right) answer. Another application is experimental graph theory, verifying whether a particular property is true for all graphs. It is much easier to believe the four-color theorem after you have demonstrated four colorings for all planar graphs on fifteen vertices.

Many factors complicate the problem of generating graphs. First, make sure you know exactly what type of graph you want to generate. Figure 7.2 on page 198 illustrates several important properties of graphs. For purposes of generation, the most important questions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Do I want labeled or unlabeled graphs?} - The issue here is whether the names of the vertices matter in deciding whether two graphs are the same. In generating \textit{labeled graphs}, we seek to construct all possible labelings of all possible graph topologies. When generating \textit{unlabeled graphs}, we want exactly one representative for each topology and ignore labelings. For example, there are only two connected unlabeled graphs on three vertices - a triangle and a simple path. However, there are four connected labeled graphs on three vertices - one triangle and three 3 -vertex paths, each distinguished by the name of their central vertex. In general, labeled graphs are much easier to generate. However, you are likely to get swamped with many isomorphic copies of the same few graphs.
\item Do I want directed or undirected graphs? - Most natural generation algorithms generate undirected graphs. These can be turned into directed graphs by flipping coins to orient the edges. Any graph can be oriented to be directed and acyclic (i.e., a DAG) by randomly permuting the vertices on a line and aiming each edge from left to right. With all such ideas, careful thought must be given to decide whether you are generating all graphs uniformly at random, and how much this matters to you.
\end{itemize}

