I will now show how to transform an instance of sorting (say $\{13, 5, 11, 17\}$) to an instance of the convex hull problem. This means we must translate each number to a point in the plane. We do so by mapping $x$ to $(x, x^{2})$. Why? This maps each integer to a point on the parabola $y = x^{2}$, as shown in Figure 11.1. Since the region above this parabola is convex, every point must be on the convex hull. Furthermore, since neighboring points on the convex hull have neighboring $x$ values, the convex hull returns the points sorted by the $x$-coordinate---that is, the original numbers. Creating and reading off these points takes $O(n)$ time:

\begin{itemize}
\item Sort($S$)
\item For each $i \in S$, create point $(i, i^{2})$.
\item Call subroutine convex-hull on this point set.
\item From the left-most point in the hull, read off the points from left to right.
\end{itemize}

What does this mean? Recall the sorting lower bound of $\Omega(n \log n)$. If we could compute convex hull in better than $n \log n$, this reduction would imply that we could sort faster than $\Omega(n \log n)$, which violates our lower bound. Thus, convex hull must take $\Omega(n \log n)$ as well! Observe that any $O(n \log n)$ convex hull algorithm also gives us a complicated but correct $O(n \log n)$ sorting algorithm when coupled with this reduction.

