TSP with repeated vertices is easily solved by using any conventional TSP code on a new cost matrix $D$, where $D(i, j)$ is the shortest path distance from $i$ to $j$. This matrix satisfies the triangle inequality, and can be constructed by solving an all-pairs shortest path (see Section 18.4 (page 554)).

\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Is your distance function symmetric?} -- A distance function is \emph{asymmetric} when there exists $x$, $y$ such that $d(x, y) \neq d(y, x)$. The asymmetric traveling salesman problem (ATSP) is much harder to solve in practice than symmetric (STSP) instances, so try to avoid such pathological distance functions. Be aware that there is a reduction converting ATSP instances to symmetric instances containing twice as many vertices [GP07]. This can be useful, because symmetric solvers are so much better.
\item \emph{How important is it to find the optimal tour?} -- Heuristic solutions will suffice for most applications. There are two different approaches if you insist on solving your TSP to optimality, however. \emph{Cutting plane methods} model the problem as an integer program, then solve the linear programming relaxation of it. Additional constraints designed to force integrality are then added if the optimal solution is not at an integer point. \emph{Branch-and-bound algorithms} perform a combinatorial search while maintaining careful upper and lower bounds on the cost of a tour. In the hands of professionals, problems with thousands of vertices can be solved. Maybe you can too, if you use the best solver available.
\end{itemize}

Almost any flavor of TSP is going to be NP-complete, so the right way to proceed is with heuristics. These typically come within a few percent of the optimal solution, which is close enough for engineering work. Literally dozens of heuristics have been proposed for TSP, so the situation can be confusing.

