\textbf{Input description}: A set of items $S=\{1,\ldots,n\}$, where item $i$ has size $s_i$ and value $v_i$. A knapsack capacity $C$.

\textbf{Problem description}: Find the subset $S'$ of $S$ that maximizes the value of $\sum_{i \in S'} v_i$, given $\sum_{i \in S'} s_i \leq C$; that is, all the items fit in a knapsack of size $C$.

\textbf{Discussion}: The knapsack problem arises in resource allocation with financial constraints. How do you select what things to buy given a fixed budget? Everything has a cost and value, so we seek the most value for the given cost. The \emph{knapsack problem} should invoke the image of the backpacker who is constrained by a fixed-size knapsack, and so must fill it with the most useful and portable items.

