\section{Graph Problems: NP-Hard}

A cynical view of graph algorithms is that "everything we want to do is hard." Indeed, all problems in this section are provably NP-complete with the exception of graph isomorphism - whose complexity status remains an open question. The theory of NP-completeness demonstrates that either \emph{all} NP-complete problems have polynomial-time algorithms, or \emph{none} of them do. The former prospect is sufficiently unlikely that NP-completeness suffices to say that no efficient algorithm exists to solve the given problem.

Still, do not abandon hope if your problem resides in this chapter. I provide a recommended attack for every problem, be it combinatorial search, heuristics, approximation algorithms, or algorithms for restricted instances. Hard problems require a different methodology to work with than polynomial-time problems, but with care can usually be dealt with successfully.

The following books will help you deal with NP-complete problems:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Garey and Johnson} [GJ79] - This is the classic reference on the theory of NP-completeness. Most notably, it contains a concise catalog of over 400 NP-complete problems, with associated references and comments. Browse through this catalog if you suspect your problem might be hard. This is the book in my algorithms library that I reach for most often.
\item \emph{Crescenzi and Kann} [CK97] - This website (www.nada.kth.se/\textasciitilde{}viggo/problemlist/) serves as the "Garey and Johnson" for approximation algorithms.
\item \emph{Williamson and Shmoys} [WS11] - The most comprehensive textbook on the theory and design of approximation algorithms.
\item \emph{Vazirani} [Vaz04] - A complete treatment of the theory of approximation algorithms by a highly regarded researcher in the field.
\item \emph{Gonzalez} [Gon18] - This handbook contains current surveys on a variety of techniques for dealing with hard problems, both applied and theoretical.
\end{itemize}

