\section{Flavors of Graphs}
A graph $G=(V, E)$ is defined on a set of \emph{vertices} $V$, and contains a set of \emph{edges} $E$ of ordered or unordered pairs of vertices from $V$. In modeling a road network, the \emph{vertices} may represent the cities or junctions, certain pairs of which are connected by roads/edges. In analyzing the source code of a computer program, the \emph{vertices} may represent lines of code, with an edge connecting lines $x$ and $y$ if $y$ is the next statement executed after $x$. In analyzing human interactions, the \emph{vertices} typically represent people, with edges connecting pairs of related souls.

Several fundamental properties of graphs impact the choice of the data structures used to represent them and algorithms available to analyze them. The first step in any graph problem is determining the flavors of the graphs that you will be dealing with (see Figure 7.2):

