We assume that any permutation $p$ is an arrangement of distinct integers $1$ trough $|p|$, so getting this right requires relabeling the elements of the smaller permutation to reflect the deleted first element. This is why $\{1,3\}$ magically turns into $\{1,2\}$ in the following example:
\[
\operatorname{Rank}(\{2,1,3\})=(2-1)\cdot 2!+\operatorname{Rank}(\{1,2\})=2+(1-1)\cdot 1!+\operatorname{Rank}(\{1\})=2
\]

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Unrank}$(m,n)$ -- Which permutation is in position $m$ of the $n!$ permutations of $n$ items? A typical unranking function finds the number of times $(n-1)!$ goes into $m$ and proceeds recursively. $\operatorname{Unrank}(2,3)$ tells us that the first element of the permutation must be ``2'', because $(2-1)\cdot(3-1)!\leq 2$ but $(3-1)\cdot(3-1)!>2$. Deleting $(2-1)\cdot(3-1)!$ from $m$ leaves the smaller problem $\operatorname{Unrank}(0,2)$. The ranking of $0$ corresponds to the total order. The total order on the two remaining elements (since 2 has been used) is $\{1,3\}$, so $\operatorname{Unrank}(2,3)=\{2,1,3\}$.
\end{itemize}

What the rank and unrank functions actually do does not matter so long as they are inverses of each other. In other words, $p=\operatorname{Unrank}(\operatorname{Rank}(p), n)$ for all permutations $p$. You can perform many different tasks once you have ranking and unranking functions for permutations:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Sequencing permutations} -- To determine the \textit{next} permutation that occurs in order after $p$, we can $\operatorname{Rank}(p)$, add 1, and then $\operatorname{Unrank}(p)$. Similarly, the permutation right before $p$ in order is $\operatorname{Unrank}(\operatorname{Rank}(p)-1,|p|)$. Counting through the integers from $0$ to $n!-1$ and unranking them is equivalent to generating all permutations.
\item \textit{Generating random permutations} -- Selecting a random integer from $0$ to $n!-1$ and then unranking it yields a truly random permutation.
\item \textit{Keep track of a set of permutations} -- Suppose we are generating random permutations, but want to act only when we encounter one we have never generated before. We can set up a bit vector (see Section 15.5 (page 456)) with $n!$ bits, and set bit $i$ to 1 if permutation $p=\operatorname{Unrank}(i,n)$ has already been seen. A similar technique was employed with $k$-subsets in the Lotto application of Section 1.8 (page 22).
\end{itemize}

