The security policy is typically given as a reference to a so-called \textit{Certificate Signing Policy (CSP)}, which amongst other things contains information about how certificates are stored by the CA and about which requirements are in place for checking the owner's identity when the certificate is issued.

The CA's signature covers all the other fields in the certificate. All parties who get hold of the certificate and who trust this CA can use the certificate's contents in the specified contexts-for example, to check that Alice has signed messages with her private key, $SK_{A}$.

\subsection{How Are Certificates Issued in a PKI?}
To issue a certificate in a PKI, the CA must get hold of the subject's public key and identity. Here there are two different scenarios, depending on where the subject's key pair is created:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Keys are created by the subject:} The subject sends the CA a \textit{Certificate Signing Request (CSR)}). The request is usually in the so-called \textit{PKCS\#10} format [69] and contains the subject's public key together with information which proves the subject's identity. It is encrypted with the subject's private key, so the receiver can check that this matches the public key. But the actual private key stays with the subject. The CA (or a separate \textit{Registration Authority (RA)}) checks the identity and generates a certificate, which is sent to the subject and typically also stored by the CA in a publicly accessible database.
  \item \textbf{Keys are created by the CA:} In this case, the CA can itself just create the certificate. After this, there are two possibilities: (1) The key pair and certificate are stored in a smartcard or similar, which is sent in a secure manner to the subject. Or (2) The key pair and certificate are sent to the subject in a file in \textit{PKCS\#12} format[66]. In this case, the key pair and certificate are typically protected by symmetric encryption with a key derived from a password. An \textit{activation code}, which is sent separately, makes it possible fot the recipient to decrypt the information and install the keys in a browser, mail client or server, depending on what the keys are to be used for.
\end{enumerate}

Obviously there are some requirements on the proof of identity. How strict these are will depend on what the keys are to be used for. In general CAs divide certificates up into three classes:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Modest requirements for proof of identity. The keys can typically only be used for signing and encrypting e-mail and for authentication of clients.
  \item Moderate requirements for proof of identity. The keys can also be used for signing documents where a higher level of trust in the identity is required.
  \item Strong requirements for proof of identity. The keys can also be used for authentication of servers. A special subclass of Class 3 certificates, known as \textit{Extended Validation (EV) certificates}, is used for servers which are to be accessed via SSL/TLS, where authentication of the server is critical.
\end{enumerate}

