\section{Exercises}
\subsection{Pattern locks}
A typical pattern lock for user authentication requires the user to draw at least 5 connected straight line segments, where each segment joins two points out of the nine which are the "basic pattern" for the lock. How many different patterns can be drawn? (Two patterns which go through the same points, but where the starting point in one pattern is the finishing point in the other, count as \emph{different} patterns.)

Suppose now that, instead of a pattern lock, you use a password which consists of a sequence of randomly chosen characters from an alphabet which only contained the 26 lower case letters (a-z) in the English alphabet. How long (measured as the number of characters) must the password be, in order for it to be possible to form more different passwords than the number of patterns?

