\section{Further reading}
Maintaining business continuity is a topic which business managers are very much focused on. It has its own practice and its own jargon. A useful source explaining the jargon is the \emph{BCI/DRJ Glossary of Terms}, compiled by the Business Continuity Institute (BCI) and the Disaster Recovery Journal (DRJ) in collaboration. It can be found at URI \href{https://www.thebci.org/resource/bci---drj-glossary-of-terms.html}{https://www.thebci.org/resource/bci---drj-glossary-of-terms.html}

A collection of articles on recovery after disasters is \emph{``IT Disaster Recovery''}, which can be found at URI \href{https://www.continuitycentral.com/itc.htm}{https://www.continuitycentral.com/itc.htm}. NIST in USA have produced an excellent, more specific report on recovery after cybersecurity events, \emph{``Guide for Cybersecurity Event Recovery''} [11].

There is a lot of literature about investigation of possible cyberattacks, both generally and in specific operating systems. NIST have produced both a general report \emph{``Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques into Incident Response''} [57] and a more specific report on investigation of mobile equipment, \emph{``Guidelines on Mobile Device Forensics''} [9]. The authorities in many countries often have local sets of instructions which specify how such forensic investigation must be carried out in order for the evidence to be valid in a subsequent court case. An example from the UK is the police guide \emph{``Good Practice Guide for Computer-Based Electronic Evidence''} [100].

