\subsection{Vulnerabilities in HTTP Authentication}
Both forms of authentication in HTTP have vulnerabilities.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Basic authentication} is very weak:
  \item Userid and password are sent in Base64 encoding, which anyone with just a modest knowledge of IT can read by converting the encoded value back to the original text. You can find small apps on the Internet which can do this.
  \item \textbf{Digest authentication} is considerably stronger:
  \item The password is sent encrypted (actually as part of a hash value).
  \item Replay is avoided by including a \textit{nonce} in the calculation of the hash value, and possibly by using a \textit{client nonce}, cnonce, and a \textit{nonce counter}, nc.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{BUT:} if no hash algorithm is specified, then MD5 is used. This is a weak hash function which is not completely collision resistent. It is strongly recommended to use a stronger algorithm, such as SHA2-256.

More fundamentally, the problem is that authentification uses a userid and password to identify the client. The method is therefore exposed to the same risks as any other authentification method based on the same approach. It also has the weakness that the client authenticates itself to the server, but not vice versa, so a client can be fooled into giving information to a false HTTP server.

If both methods are thought to be too weak, there are at least two alternatives which are notably more secure. The most obvious one is not to use HTTP but \textit{HTTPS}, so the parties can use TLS' possibilities for authentication and protection of credentials which are to be sent to and fro. Another possibility is for the parties to authenticate one another by using Kerberos, as described in Sect. 9.1.5; This solution is often preferred in Windows-based systems.

