In the computer world the central question is how one can trust that a claim made by a subject A is true or false. For example: how can you decide whether A's claim that it has a given public key $P K_{A}$ is true or false? There have been many proposals for how to answer this type of question, but they can be collected into classes which are based on different \emph{trust models}.

Basically, a trust model describes the rules for how trust between subjects arises and can be checked. Three commonly used models are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The hierarchical trust model.
  \item The web-of-trust model.
  \item The reputation-based trust model
\end{enumerate}

These three models are discussed below.

\subsection{The Hierarchical Trust Model}
This model is based on a hierarchy of authorities (CAs), which sign certificates and thus issue a guarantee that a particular subject owns the public key which is presented in the certificate. Subjects further down in the hierarchy trust certificates which have been signed by CAs higher up. The classic example of this is the \emph{PKI systems} described above.

To decide whether subject A has the the public key $P K_{A}$ according to the hierarchical trust model, you must be able to trust the CA which signed A's certificate. This is easy if your own certificate comes from the same CA. But if you do not directly know whether you trust the CA, you must be able to trust a CA which is a common superior for your own and A's CA in the hierarchy. Or, if A's CA belongs to a different hierarchy, you have to trust the root CA of that other hierarchy. This is illustrated in Fig. 5.17.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{2025_05_12_ac3bc46a6f93dd973caag-131}
\caption{Two trust hierarchies in a PKI}
\end{figure}

In the figure, circles represent CAs, and rounded boxes represent subjects. Arrows indicate the direction of trust—showing for example that subject B trusts $C A_{2}$, and $C A_{4}$ trusts $C A_{0}$. The small yellow stars represent certificates, which in all cases have been signed by the CA one level higher up in the hierarchy. So for example E's certificate has been signed by $C A_{3}$ and $C A_{3}$'s certificate by $C A_{1}$. And since subject A's certificate has been signed by $C A_{2}$ and F's certificate by $C A_{4}$, then A and F can trust one another's certificates, since they have a common superior, $C A_{0}$, in the hierarchy. $C A_{0}$ and $C A_{5}$ are the two root CAs, as described in Sect. 5.5. They have root certificates, shown as small red stars.

