\subsection{Encryption in the Operating System}
An alternative way of protecting data against unwanted readers is to \emph{encrypt} the data, so it becomes unimportant whether an unauthorised person or process gains access to an area where sensitive data is stored-it will anyway not be possible to understand or exploit these data unless you have the necessary decryption functions and keys.

Encryption for protecting stored data can be introduced in different ways. Three of the most common are:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Through software functions built in to the operating system, such as BitLocker for Windows and FileVault for OS/X;
  \item Through software functions in a utility program for the OS, such as TrueCrypt (now discontinued) and its successors VeraCrypt and CipherShed;
  \item By using encryption units built into the hardware in the IT system's disks. These units are controlled by drivers installed in the OS.
\end{enumerate}

Encryption systems in the operating system normally aim to provide \emph{transparent} \emph{encryption}-that is to say, data are automatically decrypted when they are read and automatically encrypted when they are written.

Some encryption systems only give the user the possibility of encrypting individual files or folders (such as in Windows with EFS, but without BitLocker), while others make it possible to encrypt the whole disk, a technique known as \emph{Full Disk Encryption} (or just \emph{FDE} for short). FDE has the advantage that almost everything on the disk can be encrypted-i.e. not just the data content of ordinary files, but also the files' so-called \emph{metadata} (the files' names, sizes, creation and modification times etc.) and any areas on the disk used as \emph{swap areas}, where parts of programs which are active, but not currently in the computer's memory, are temporarily stored. Both metadata and swap areas can contain confidential information which an attacker perhaps can exploit. They are normally not protected by systems which only allow encryption of individual files and folders.

