\subsection{Addressing}
Just like when something has to be sent for example by post, the destination (and source) for a PDU is identified by an \emph{address}, whose form depends on the layer.

For example in the Internet:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Application Layer:} An \emph{Internet name}, which identifies a system, e.g.: \href{http://odin.compute.dtu.dk}{odin.compute.dtu.dk}.
  \item \textbf{Transport Layer:} A \emph{port number}, in the interval [0..65535], which identifies a client or server in a given system, e.g.: 80 for an HTTP server.
  \item \textbf{Internet Layer :} An \emph{IP address}, which identifies a hardware network interface, e.g.: 130.225.76.4 for use with IP version 4.
\end{itemize}

There can be several network interfaces in a physical computer system – for example, if the system works as a \emph{router}, which can direct traffic to and from several neighbour networks. So a system can correspondingly have several IP addresses.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Link layer:} A \emph{MAC address}, which also identifies a network interface, e.g.: 49-bd-d2-c7-56-2a. This address is used by the specific hardware which is used in the Link layer, and its form therefore depends on the technology.
\end{itemize}

In general, a system will only accept PDUs which are addressed to itself. However, it is often possible to set up a computer as a so-called \emph{sniffer} which accepts everything which comes past. Sniffers can be used for monitoring the network, where they serve an important, legitimate purpose. But they can also be used by attackers to gain unauthorised access to (possibly confidential) data.

