\subsection{Updating the Operating System}
Vulnerabilities in OSs are regularly discovered and need to be repaired or removed. All reputable OS suppliers send out corrections, which the system administrator must install. In many organisations this is, however, not a trivial task, as (especially in large organisations) the deployed versions of the OS are not $100 \%$ alike.

Failure to update the OS will typically leave the way open for attacks from malicious programs of various sorts: \emph{Viruses, worms, spyware, adware, Trojan horses, . . .}. A spectacular example of this was the spreading of the ransomware worm \emph{WannaCry} in Spring 2017. This malware infected unupdated computers (typically with an older version of Windows such as Windows XP, 7 or 8) in at least 150 countries, and affected private citizens, companies and critical functions of society such as hospitals and railways.

Mitre Corporation i USA maintains a database of vulnerabilities which have been reported through the \emph{CVE system}\footnote{CVE is an abbreviation for \emph{Common Vulnerabilties and Exposures}.}, and which have therefore become publicly known. Figure 9.22, which is based on data from Mitre, shows the number of vulnerabilities reported in the years 2005-2020. Until 2016 the number of reported (and therefore presumably new) vulnerabilities was roughly constant around 6000 a year, but since then the number has increased considerably and is now approaching 20000. And as soon as information about a vulnerability is made public, very little time passes-in some cases just a couple of days-before it is exploited by attackers.

In reality there are almost certainly many more vulnerabilities, either in the OS or other system programs, which have not been publicly reported. This is demonstrated quite clearly by a number of high-profile zero-day attacks, i.e. attacks which exploit a vulnerability, which until now has been unkown, in the victim's system. This has become a good business opportunity for criminals, who can discover these vulnerabilities and sell their knowledge to the actual attackers. Unfortunately it lies in the nature of things that software updates usually come too late to protect against just exactly this form of attack.

