\subsection{Trends in User Authentication}
There are a number of risks associated with only using a single method for user authentication. Firstly, because some methods do not give a 100\% perfect identification. And secondly, because things which you have can be stolen or in some cases copied, things which you know can be disclosed by social engineering or surveillance and even some things which you are can be imitated. To reduce the risk of faulty user authentication, the modern trend is therefore to combine several authentification methods. This is known as \textit{multi-factor authentication}. There are many common combinations for use in different contexts, including:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Something you \textbf{have} + something you \textbf{know}. \textit{E.g. Credit card chip + PIN code}
  \item Something you \textbf{know} + something you \textbf{are}. \textit{E.g. Password + fingerprint}
  \item Something you \textbf{have} + something you \textbf{are}. \textit{E.g. Smartcard + iris recognition}
\end{enumerate}

The third type of combination with a hardware device and a biometric has the advantage that it does not require users to remember passwords or PIN codes, and is the technical basis for the \textit{passkeys} standard promoted by the World Wide Web Consortium. The process may be made even more secure if the system, after checking the validity of one or more factors, requires the user to send some form of one-time password (OTP), as described above. This makes it much more difficult for an attacker to collect up all the information needed in order to be accepted as the genuine user, and is a common practice in systems where establishment of personal identity is very important, such as banking systems and systems based on eIDs.

\subsection{Authentication Through the Net}
When users have to authenticate themselves to an IT system through a network, all information which is to be used as evidence of identity must be protected against eavesdropping or replaying. There are two common strategies for this:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Send authentication information through a protected-that is to say, encrypted channel. This strategy is for example used in software for secure login based on the protocol \textit{Secure Shell (SSH)}, a widely used product in operating systems based on Unix. Server-based systems where access to the server is secured by the use of SSL/TLS (as described in Sect. 8.4) also use this approach, so any necessary credentials can be sent securely to the server.
\end{enumerate}

