\textbf{Functions:} A function describes the relationship between members of a set of inputs, say $X$, to members of a set of possible outputs, say $Y$. The set of possible inputs is known as the function's \textit{domain} and the set of possible outputs as the function's \textit{co-domain}. Then we can define the function by giving the rule which identifies the element in $Y$ which will be the output when the input is a particular element, $x$, of $X$.

For example: The function definition $f(x)=2 x$ defines a function whose output is the integer which is twice as large as the value of the input, while $f(x)=2 x+1$ gives as output the integer which is one larger than twice the input value. Such relationships are often visualised by drawing the \textit{graph} of the function which shows the correspondance between each of the elements in the set of inputs and the relevant output. An example can be seen in Fig. B.1.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[max width=\textwidth, center]{2025_05_12_ac3bc46a6f93dd973caag-410}
\caption{Graph of the function $f(x)=2 x+1$ with domain the set of integers $\{1,2,3,4,5\}$ and co-domain the set of all non-negative integers less than 20.}
\end{figure}

The element $f(x)$ in the codomain is often known as the \textit{image of} $x$ (under $f$) or the \textit{value of} $f$ \textit{at} $x$ and the set of images for all the elements in the domain is known as the \textit{image of the function} $f$. For example: In the figure, 7 is the image of 3, and the image of $f$ is the set of integers $\{3,5,7,9,11\}$. By definition, since $f(x)$ is a function, each element in its domain has \textit{at most} one image. This means that $f(x)$ for a given $x$ can only produce a unique output, if any output is defined for it at all. In fact, in the example in the figure, all the elements in the domain have one image, as $f$ is a so-called \textit{total function}, but in other cases some of the elements in the domain may not have any outputs defined—for example, the function $f(x)=1 / x$ over the domain of integers has no defined value at $x=0$.

