\section{Further Reading}
For readers without any special technical background, Douglas Comer's book \emph{"The Internet Book: Everything you need to know about computer networking and how the Internet works"}, now in its 4th. edition from 2007 [15], gives a good introduction to the Internet and how it works. Two books with more technical detail are Comer's \emph{"Computer Networks and Internets"}, now in its 6th. edition from 2014 [16], and Kurose and Ross's \emph{"Computer Networking-A Top-Down Approach Featuring the Internet"}, now in its 7th. edition from 2016 [60].

For even more details about how the Internet works it is necessary to consult the Internet's own documentation, which is assembled from a large number of so-called \emph{RFCs}, each of which is identified by a number. For example, RFC793 describes in detail how TCP works. RFC stands for \emph{Request for Comments}, and the typical RFC is a proposed description of a protocol for use in the Internet. As the name implies, interested parties can comment on it and suggest changes or corrections. Eventually this process may lead to the publication of a new, revised RFC, which gets a new number. If after 6 months there is general agreement on the text, the RFC is given a status of \emph{Draft Standard}. After at least another 4 months, it can be promoted to an \emph{Internet Standard}, where it gets an extra number which identifies it in the series of full standards (STDs). RFC793, for example, is \emph{Internet Standard STD7}. If a modified version of the protocol is developed at a later date, it gets a completely new RFC number. The current status of all RFCs is published at regular intervals in a catalogue which (of course) itself is an RFC, published as STD1, with the title \emph{"Internet Protocol Standards"}. It is easy to find the RFCs on the Internet. The RFC editor maintains a website, \href{https://www.rfc-editor.org}{https://www.rfc-editor.org}, where it is possible to search for RFCs using various criteria, such as the RFC's number or status, or by a search based on keywords.

