\subsection{SessionIDs}
A \emph{SessionID} is a set of authentication data which is stored on the server and used to check that subsequent requests from the client are part of the same transaction. A simple example:

\begin{itemize}
  \item The user starts a web application. The client sends the first HTTP request (typically without a \emph{SessionID}).
  \item The server asks for the user's userid and password.
  \item The client sends the user's userid and password to the server.
  \item The server sends the client a \emph{SessionID} for the transaction.
  \item Each subsequent request from the client includes this \emph{SessionID}, so the server can check that the request belongs to a valid transaction.
\end{itemize}

SessionIDs can be transferred in three different ways:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The \emph{SessionID} can be part of a query in the URI which the client uses: \href{http://www.wshop.dk/bigbuy?sessionID=13EDFA256}{http://www.wshop.dk/bigbuy?sessionID=13EDFA256}
