\subsection{Asymmetric Cryptosystems}
Asymmetric cryptosystems are characterised by the fact that everyone who uses the cryptosystem has both a secret and a public key, which is why they are also known as \emph{Public Key Cryptosystems (PKCS)}. Here it is important to know which party we are talking about, so it is traditional to give them characteristic names. For example for two parties: Alice and Bob. Suppose they have keys as follows:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\textbf{Alice} & \textbf{Bob} \\
\hline
Secret key: $SK_{A}$ & Secret key: $SK_{B}$ \\
Public key: $PK_{A}$ & Public key: $PK_{B}$ \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The \emph{public keys} are in principle known by everybody. If one party, Alice (who in reality could be a person or merely a process in a computer) wants to keep some information confidential for everyone else except a particular party, Bob, then she uses Bob's \emph{public key}, $PK_{B}$, to encrypt the information. Only Bob (again: in principle) knows his own \emph{secret key}, $SK_{B}$, which can be used to decrypt the information. This key is therefore also known as Bob's \emph{private key}. These properties are illustrated in Fig. 4.17.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[max width=\textwidth, center]{2025_05_12_ac3bc46a6f93dd973caag-094}
\caption{Encryption and decryption in a PKCS}
\end{figure}

It is important to notice that it must not be possible from publicly available knowledge to compute the inverse function $\mathcal{E}^{-1}$, which reverses the effect of $\mathcal{E}$. Technically speaking, the encryption function $\mathcal{E}$ in a PKCS must be a so-called \emph{trapdoor function}.

