\begin{itemize}
  \item The client sends the Ticket Granting Server the received, encrypted TGT, an identification of the desired service (SID), and an \textit{authenticator}, \textit{Authenticator}$_1$, which contains UID and a timestamp, encrypted with $SK_1$.
  \item The server (which knows $SK_{TGS}$) decrypts TGT and thus finds $SK_1$, which it uses to decrypt the authenticator. Then it sends the client a new ticket (TT), which the client can use in relation to the Service Server, together with a new session key ($SK_2$), encrypted with $SK_1$. TT has a limited period of validity and is encrypted with a key $SK_{TS}$, which is only known by the Ticket Granting Server and Service Server.
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
  \setcounter{enumi}{2}
  \item Using the chosen service, which involves three steps:
\end{enumerate}

\begin{itemize}
  \item The client sends the Service Server the ticket TT and a new authenticator (\textit{Authenticator}$_2$), which contains UID and a timestamp, encrypted with $SK_2$.
  \item The Service Server (which knows $SK_{TS}$) decrypts TT and thus finds the session key $SK_2$, which it uses to decrypt the authenticator to find the timestamp. Then it sends the timestamp, encrypted with $SK_2$, back to the client to confirm that it will provide the desired service.
  \item The client decrypts this confirmation and checks that the timestamp is correct. If it is, then the client can trust the server and can begin to request services.
\end{itemize}

It is very important for the security of Kerberos that the servers' secret keys ($SK_{TGS}$ and $SK_{TS}$) are not disclosed, and that all timestamps are reliable. This latter requirement means that the clocks in all the computers must be synchronised, for example by using a standard protocol for clock synchronisation, such as \textit{NTP}. It is also important that the symmetric encryption algorithm chosen for use is sufficiently strong. Although it is mandatory to implement AES256-CTS\footnote{CTS—CipherText Stealing—is a variant of the CBC mode for block ciphers, in which the ciphertext has exactly the same length as the plaintext, instead of filling a whole number of blocks.}, and it is recommended to offer AES128-CTS as well, weaker encryption algorithms such as DES-CBC can be found in older implementations of Kerberos. Their use is now deprecated.

Kerberos is widely used, and it is the default authentication method in Windows systems since Windows 2000. One of the reasons for its widespread use is that it makes it easy to set up a system for so-called \textit{single sign-on (SSO)}. Many large organisations have numerous services which in principle are independent of one another, but which all use the same credentials for authentication of users, who can therefore avoid having to remember different passwords for different services. For the users, this is a great relief, and it improves security because the users do not suffer from so-called \textit{password tiredness}, so they fall into bad habits such as writing the passwords down in plaintext in order to remember them. On the other hand, it can be very irritating to have to repeat the authentication process with the \textit{same} credentials, if you want to use several services. SSO makes it possible to authenticate yourself just once, after which you have access to all the available services. In an SSO system based on Kerberos, authentication (Phase 1) only needs to be performed once, while Phases 2 and 3 can be repeated for each of the services which the client wants to use, within the limits set by the validity periods of the individual tickets. The negative aspect of SSO is naturally that you only have one set of credentials for everything, so loss, disclosure or theft of the credentials will have very serious consequences.

