\subsection{Buffer Overflow Vulnerabilities}
Buffer overflow is typically a problem when software is written in languages such as \textit{C}, which do not automatically check whether there is space for data which is moved round in the computer's memory. Such languages are often chosen for programming system programs, because they make it possible to develop relatively efficient software through optimisations in speed and memory consumption. Part of this advantage arises just exactly because automatic checks for buffer overflow are missing-checks cost both time and space. The downside of this is an increased risk of vulnerabilities.

A classic example is the string handling functions in C , which for example are used for copying texts:

\begin{itemize}
  \item strcpy(dst, src); [Copies from src to dst]
\end{itemize}

This function is \textit{unsafe}, as there is no check for whether the buffer dst can hold the contents of the buffer src.

\begin{itemize}
  \item strncpy(dst, src, count)
\end{itemize}

This function copies at most count characters from src to dst. Use of this function is guaranteed not to cause buffer overflow, if count is set equal to the size of the buffer dst, like this:\\
strncpy(dst, src, sizeof dst)\\
Unfortunately the safe variant strncpy is confusing for the programmer, as the count argument has another meaning than in, say, the function strncat, which appends src to the end of dst. Inexperienced software developers therefore often choose the unsafe variant, because they can't quite remember how the safe variant should be used. There are corresponding problems with the other string handling functions in C, including functions for inputting text, such as fgets and scanf. In recent years, so-called \textit{safe libraries} for C and other languages have been developed, so these vulnerabilities are no longer present. But by no means all development projects make use of this improvement.

