\subsection{Diffie-Hellman Key Agreement}
Diffie and Hellman's protocol for agreeing on a shared secret was the first practical solution to the problem of distributing keys via an insecure communication channel. Given a publicly known large prime number $q$ and a publicly known integer $\alpha$ (pronounced ``alfa''), Alice and Bob each have a secret key, $x_{A}$ and $x_{B}$ respectively, and send one another information as follows:
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Message 1} & A \rightarrow B: \alpha^{x_{A}} \bmod q \\
\text{Message 2} & B \rightarrow A: \alpha^{x_{B}} \bmod q
\end{array}
\]

Alice and Bob can then calculate the same \textit{shared secret} --- $K=\alpha^{x_{A} \cdot x_{B}} \bmod q$ --- to be used, for example, as the key in an SKCS.

Essentially, Diffie--Hellman agreement relies on a PKCS where $x_{A}$ and $x_{B}$ are Alice's and Bob's respective secret keys, while ($\alpha^{x_{A}} \bmod q$) and ($\alpha^{x_{B}} \bmod q$) are their public keys, just as in the Digital Signature Algorithm. Since it is very difficult to find the exponent $x$, if you only know ($\alpha^{x} \bmod q$), $\alpha$ and $q$, it is infeasible for Bob (or an attacker Eve who is eavesdropping on the exchange) to find Alice's secret key, $x_{A}$, and infeasible for Alice (or Eve) to find Bob's secret key, $x_{B}$. An important consequence of this is that \textit{passive attackers}, who just listen to the exchange, cannot derive the shared secret.

On the other hand, the protocol is not secure against \textit{active attackers}, such as Mallet, who can perform a man-in-the-middle attack which inserts or changes messages on their way between Alice and Bob, as shown in Fig. 5.14. Mallet replaces Alice's message $\alpha^{x_{A}}$ with $\alpha^{x_{A}^{\prime}}$ and Bob's reply $\alpha^{x_{B}}$ with $\alpha^{x_{B}^{\prime}}$, where both $x_{A}^{\prime}$ and $x_{B}^{\prime}$ are numbers which Mallet has chosen. This means that Alice calculates the key $K_{A}=\alpha^{x_{A} \cdot x_{B}^{\prime}}$, while Bob calculates $K_{B}=\alpha^{x_{B} \cdot x_{A}^{\prime}}$. But Mallet has all the information needed to calculate both these keys. He can therefore decrypt all messages sent later from Alice to Bob or vice versa, re-encrypt them using the key which the intended receiver has calculated, and then forward them to the intended receiver. Thus Alice and Bob's conversation is compromised without either of them being aware of it.

