\subsection{Communication in the Internet}
The Internet is based on a reference model with five layers, which are shown in Fig. 6.4. The four lowest layers together provide a communication service which permits transfer of data between processes which are running in arbitrary systems connected to the network:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Physical:} transfers signals in the form of changing electrical voltages, light pulses, or radio waves in a medium which directly connects two systems.
  \item \textbf{Link:} Transfers data in blocks between directly connected systems. "Directly" means here that there is a cable or optic fibre which goes directly from one system to another, or that both systems have access to a \textit{shared medium}, for example based on radio communication.
  \item \textbf{Internet:} permits data transfer to arbitrary systems ("nodes") in a possibly very complex, composed communication network.
  \item \textbf{Transport:} gives the illusion of direct \textit{end-to-end} transfers between processes in arbitrary systems, in the sense that the communicating processes cannot see the route or other details of how the network has been traversed. As there may be many processes running in a host at a given moment in time, the Transport layer also needs ro make it possible for several separate streams of data can pass through the same connection in the Internet layer.
\end{itemize}

