\subsection{Security with Java Applets}
When Java applets are used, security is based on a so-called \textit{sandbox} model: The applet is executed in a very restrictive environment (in a JVM) with three important components:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Security Manager:} controls access to resources in accordance with an explicit security policy which relates resources to the corresponding permissions.
  \item \textbf{Byte Code Verifier:} checks code before it can be executed, in order to ensure that it fulfils certain quality requirements, for example:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item No stack overflow
    \item No illegal type conversions
    \item No illegal references to other Java classes.
  \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Class Loaders}: fetch class files and check that they are mutually consistent and do not disobey Java's rules.
\end{itemize}

