work as \textit{routers}, with the typical functionality that they:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Implement layers up to (at least) the Internet layer.
  \item Can choose a suitable route for sending an incoming PDU off towards its destination. The route can be fixed or be chosen dynamically to depend on the traffic.
  \item Can possibly filter off irrelevant or unsuitable traffic, for example:
  \item Traffic which has taken too long to travel through the network.
  \item Traffic from known unreliable sources.
  \item Traffic on incoming links which do not lead to the claimed source address.
  \item Traffic to destination systems or applications that do not want it.
  \item Traffic which misuses the protocols in one way or another.
\end{itemize}

This filtering function is an important element in a \textit{firewall}, which is a network component intended to protect the network's users against unwanted traffic. We discuss firewalls in more detail in Sect. 8.6 of Chap. 8.

A router which connects two networks or subnets with different properties, such as different network technologies, protocols or the like, is often called a \textit{gateway}. The wireless routers which are found in many home networks are in practice examples of gateways, as they connect the home's wireless or cable-based LAN with a network set up by an Internet service provider (an \textit{ISP}), which will typically be based on the telephone network. An example can be seen in Fig. 6.13.

It is in the nature of things that the typical router has at least one incoming and several outgoing links to physical media. But sometimes there is a need for a unit with just one incoming and one outgoing link. This type of unit is known as a \textit{bridge}. It does not offer any real routing function, but is typically used for:

\begin{itemize}
  \item Converting between different conventions which are followed in the Physical layer in different segments of the network—for example if the medium in one segment is a coaxial cable which carries electrical signals, while the neighbouring segment uses a fiberoptic medium which carries signals as pulses of light.
  \item Filtering to remove traffic which does not need to go over the bridge to reach its destination.
\end{itemize}

