\subsection{AES}
Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is a more modern encryption method than DES, approved as standard \emph{FIPS 197} by NIST in USA in 2001. It is in fact a subset of a family of algorithms known as \emph{Rijndael}, developed by two Belgian cryptographers, Joan Daemen and Vincent Rijmen. \emph{Rijndael} was the winner of a public competition to develop a new encryption algorithm to replace DES—in the first instance for encryption of non-classified material. The competition rules stipulated that the algorithm must be publicly available and for worldwide use free of royalties. It was also expected that it should be possible to implement it in a simple way on multiple platforms, both in hardware and software.

AES is a block cipher with block size 128 bits and key size 128, 192 or 256 bits. (\emph{Rijndael} had the possibility of having both block and key sizes which were 128, 160, 192, 224 or 256 bits.) It uses several \emph{rounds} of transformation, where each round uses its own 128-bit \emph{round key}, which like the partial keys in DES are derived from the main key by a systematic \emph{key schedule}. The rounds do not use Feistel networks. Instead, each round consists of four steps which transform the data block, which is considered as a $4 \times 4$ matrix of bytes, as shown in Fig. 4.12.

