\section{Chapter 10}
\section{Software security}
Development of correct, flaw-free software is a surprisingly demanding discipline. The apparently endless stream of updates which must correct errors and omissions, in both operating systems and applications, shows how difficult it is. And a considerable fraction of these updates are not just corrections for aesthetic errors, but are what software companies call \emph{critical updates}-that is to say corrections for flaws which give vulnerabilities that can threaten the computer's security. In this chapter we look at some of the most common types of software-related vulnerabilities and at some of the methods which can counteract them.

\subsection{Classes of Security Failure in Software}
A \emph{security failure} in software is basically an unwanted program behaviour caused by a \emph{program vulnerability}. These vulnerabilities can arise in two very different ways:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Non-malicious flaws}, which are introduced in the software as a result of the programmer overlooking or forgetting something. This includes flaws such as:
\end{enumerate}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Buffer overflow (or \emph{buffer overwrite}), where the software does not prevent attempts to store data in a buffer which is too small to hold them;
  \item Buffer overread, where the software does not prevent attempts to read data beyond the limits of the buffer;
  \item Incomplete mediation, where the software allows unsuitable or meaningless data to be processed;
  \item Time-of-check-to-time-of-use (TOCTTOU) flaws, where the sofware allows operations to be carried out with rights which are no longer valid.
\end{itemize}

\begin{enumerate}
  \setcounter{enumi}{1}
  \item \textbf{Malicious flaws}, which are introduced on purpose, possibly by exploiting a vulnerability arising from a non-malicious flaw. Malicious flaws are a feature of harmful programs such as viruses, worms, Trojan horses and other forms of so-called \emph{malware}, which are distributed by troublemakers and criminals.
\end{enumerate}

In this section we look at the non-malicious flaws, while \emph{malware} is discussed in Sect. 10.2.

\subsection{Buffer Overflow}
