\subsection{DES}
\textit{Data Encryption Standard (DES)} has been a standard method for symmetric encryption since 1977, when it was published as the standard \textit{FIPS PUB 46} in USA. It was developed by a team from IBM in response to an invitation to industry to send in proposals for a new encryption method which could be used for encrypting confidential information which had not been classified as secret (or the like) by military or other US government organisations. Over the years, it has been widely used for encrypting commercial information, especially for money transfers by banks.

DES is a block cipher, which encrypts data in 64-bit blocks. The key has 56 bits and is used as the basis for deriving 16 48-bit partial keys for use in the following transformations. The partial keys are derived from the main key in a systematic way described by a \textit{key schedule}. Each block is passed through a series of 19 transformation steps, as shown in Fig. 4.8. The first step is a transposition of the bits in the block. Then follow 16 Feistel rounds, where the $i$'th round uses the $i$'th partial key. After the 16th Feistel round, the two halves of the block are swapped over, and the entire transformation ends with a transposition step, which is the inverse of the first step.

This may sound amazingly complicated, but really it is just a monoalphabetic substitution cipher, where each symbol in the “alphabet” is a 64-bit bit sequence. So the “alphabet” consists of all possible sequences of 64 bits. It can therefore in principle be attacked like any other monoalphabetic substitution cipher. As early as 1999, the Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF) succeeded in decrypting a DES-encrypted message in 22 hours, by using a purpose-built machine known as \textit{Deep Crack}, which only cost 250000 USD—a quite moderate sum for a government intelligence agency or a criminal organisation. With the fast computers available today, this time can be reduced to an hour or less, so DES’ 56-bit key is much too small for real security. The use of DES is therefore strongly deprecated for uses where confidentiality of data is important.

To achieve better security while keeping the building blocks from DES, it is common to use \textit{Triple-DES (3DES)} with three different keys:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& e=\operatorname{DES}\left(\operatorname{DES}^{-1}(\operatorname{DES}(d, k_1), k_2), k_3\right) \\
& d=\operatorname{DES}^{-1}\left(\operatorname{DES}\left(\operatorname{DES}^{-1}(e, k_3), k_2\right), k_1\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

(Here notations like $\operatorname{DES}(m, k)$ mean that message $m$ is encrypted with key $k$, while $\operatorname{DES}^{-1}(m, k)$ means that $m$ is decrypted with key $k$.) For example, for encryption $e$ is found by first encrypting $d$ with $k_1$, then decrypting with $k_2$ and then encrypting with $k_3$. Triple-DES’s key is effectively of 168 bits (three 56-bit DES keys) and the security is about the same as could be achieved with DES, if it could be used with a 112-bit key.

