\subsection{Polyalphabetic Ciphers}
As monoalphabetic ciphers turned out to be relatively easy to decrypt with the help of frequency analysis, cryptographers began as early as the 15th century to investigate \textit{polyalphabetic ciphers}, where several reorderings of the alphabet are used, depending on the character's position in the plaintext.

\textbf{Example:} Encryption via cyclic permutation, where $n=5$ for \textit{odd} positions and $n=19$ for \textit{even} positions:

